ent, no other country adopted newborn circum-
cision.
The first serious questioning of this practice
did not occur until late 1949 (in England) with
the publication of Cairdner''s "The Fate of the
Foreskin,"!" which began to affect the practice
of circumcision by the British. In 1963, an ed-
itorial in].A.M.A. called the attitude of the med-
ical profession paradoxical and confused, and ad-
mitted that the facts about circumcision were
still unknown." This was followed by several
critiques of circumcision, such as those by Mor-
gan (1965 and 1967)38,39 and Preston (1970).45 In
1968, 0ster confirmed Cairdner''s findings," as
did Reichelderfer and Fraga;" who presented a
comprehensive study of circumcision. Yet some
physicians continued to support circumcision for
surprising reasons. For example, Dr. Robert P.
Bolande, writing in The New England Journal
of Medicine in 1969, compared circumcision
with tonsillectomy, calling both procedures "ri-
tualistic," and "widely performed on a non-sci-
entific basis."5 He opposed routine tonsillec-
tomy but concluded vis-a-vis circumcision:
"Little serious objection can actually be raised
against neonatal circumcision, since its adverse
effects seem minuscule."5
In the 1970''s, a change seemed in the offing.
In 1971 and 1975, the American Academy of
Pediatrics Task Force on Circumcision declared:
". . . there are no valid medical indications for
circumcision in the neonatal period."> In 1978,
the position of the American Academy of Pedi-
atrics was endorsed by The American College
of Obstetricians and Oynecologists.?'' In 1983
both groups jointly reaffirmed their positions.1
These "firm" declarations should have caused a
marked drop in the United States circumcision
rate. They did not.
To explore the circumcision rate in the United
States, it is essential to compare the American
experience with the other English-speaking
countries. Anticircumcision articles appeared in
the medical press in all of these countries.
Cairdner-? and 0ster42 were published in jour-
nals in England; Morgan (1967) was published,
in Australia" In 1971, the Australian Paediatric
Association recommended: "Male infants should
not as a routine be circumcised. "13 In 1975, the
Canadian Paediatric Society stated, ". . . there
is no medical indication for circumcision in the
neonatal period. "57
Although there are no precise data on circum-
cision from any country, approximated rates for
the English-speaking countries (Table 1) reveal
that in Great Britain, the practice has virtually
been abandoned; New Zealand follows closely


