number of sexually transmitted diseases.” Gisselquist argues that many cases of HIV in Africa are blood-borne: Gisselquist D,
Points to Consider: Responses to HIV/AIDS in Africa, Asia, and the Caribbean (Adonis & Abbey, London, 2007). The author
also hypothesises that circumcised men, having lost sensitivity, and perhaps believing that they are protected against HIV, may
be less likely to use condoms and otherwise to practise safe sex.
