1 
 
                                               
 
 
Further study needed of the impact of early-life 
surgery with associated general anesthesia on 
the risk of attention deficit hyperactivity 
disorder (ADHD) 
 
This is a commentary accepted for publication in Anesthesia & Analgesia. Author’s 
copy. It may be cited as follows: 
 
Frisch M, Earp BD, Van Howe RS. Further study needed of the impact of early-life 
surgery with associated general anesthesia on the risk of attention deficit 
hyperactivity disorder (ADHD). Anesth Analg 2021 (in press). 
 
 
Morten Frisch, MD, PhD, DSc(Med) 
Department of Epidemiology Research, Statens Serum Institut, Copenhagen S, Denmark; 
Department of Clinical Medicine, Center for Sexological Research, Aalborg University, Aalborg, Denmark 
 
Brian D. Earp, MSc (Oxford), MPhil (Cambridge), MA (Yale), MPhil (Yale) 
Yale-Hastings Program in Ethics and Health Policy, Yale University, New Haven, Connecticut; 
The Hastings Center, Garrison, New York; 
Uehiro Centre for Practical Ethics, University of Oxford, Oxford, UK 
 
Robert S. Van Howe, MD, MS 
Department of Pediatrics and Human Development, Michigan State University, College of Human Medicine 
 
 


2 
 
Ing et al should be commended for their work in two recent studies published in the Journal, in which they 
made methodologically sound attempts to address the potential impact of surgery with associated general 
anesthesia on the risk of subsequent diagnoses of, or medication use for, attention deficit hyperactivity 
disorder (ADHD) and other mental illnesses in 0-4 year-old children from Texas and New York.1,2  
 
Together, the two studies strongly suggest that common surgical procedures, which are usually carried out 
under general anesthesia during infancy or early childhood, such as pyloromyotomy, inguinal hernia repair, 
postneonatal circumcision and tonsillectomy and/or adenoidectomy, are associated with an increased risk 
of ADHD. As the authors rightly acknowledge, it is currently not possible from available data to determine 
whether the association is because of harmful effects of surgery per se or/and associated traumatic 
experiences in the perioperative period or because of the general anesthesia that accompanies most, but 
not all, of these operations. Further study is needed to clarify this. 
 
Toward this end, we would like to bring attention to a methodological issue in the two studies that may 
usefully be addressed in such further study. This issue, we suggest, may have tempered the results in boys, 
resulting in an underestimate of the HRs. In both studies,1,2 Ing et al disregarded all circumcisions 
performed during the birth admission, treating all neonatally circumcised boys as ‘unexposed’ (Ing C, 
personal communication). This is potentially problematic, since 55-64 percent of male neonates in Texas 
and 61-69 percent of those born in New York underwent circumcision during the birth hospitalization in the 
study period 1999-2010.3 By including neonatally circumcised boys in the ‘unexposed’ category, this 
reference group was not free of potentially traumatic surgical experiences. 
 
To the extent that early-life surgery per se or/and associated traumatic experiences and potential 
complications account for the observed association with ADHD risk, a possibility admitted by Ing et al in 
both papers,1,2 the authors’ decision to consider boys who underwent neonatal circumcision as ‘unexposed’ 
will have produced HR estimates that are too conservative in boys. Accordingly, the higher HR for ADHD 
among girls than boys undergoing one of the studied surgeries relevant to both sexes (girls: HR, 1.56 (95% 
CI, 1.38-1.76); boys: HR, 1.37 (95% CI, 1.28-1.47)) might reflect underestimation of the effect among boys 
by the inclusion of neonatally circumcised boys in the reference category of ‘unexposed’ boys. 
 
We would encourage Ing et al to rerun the following two analyses from their most recent paper2 and add a 
new analysis to examine the relevance and potential impact of our above-mentioned concerns.  
 


3 
 
Firstly, the authors report an overall HR of 1.37 (95% CI, 1.30-1.44) for persistent ADHD medication use 
after surgeries requiring general anesthesia among boys and girls combined (Figure 3, Panel A). What 
would this HR be, if neonatally circumcised boys (i.e., those who were circumcised during the birth 
admission) were excluded, thus restricting the ‘unexposed’ category to children who truly did not undergo 
any surgery? 
 
Secondly, in the surgery-specific analysis, the authors report a HR of 1.23 (95% CI, 1.10-1.38) among boys 
for persistent ADHD medication use after postneonatal circumcision (Figure 3, Panel D). What would this 
HR be after exclusion of neonatally circumcised boys from the ‘unexposed’ category?  
 
Thirdly, we kindly ask the authors to calculate a HR for persistent ADHD medication use, comparing hazard 
rates among boys undergoing postneonatal circumcision (approximately 91% of which required general 
anesthesia)4 with the hazard rates among boys undergoing neonatal circumcision (the vast majority of 
which are carried out without general anesthesia). The latter novel analysis is likely to provide important 
clues for determining whether it is the surgery per se or/and associated traumatic experiences and 
potential complications, the general anesthesia, or a combination of the two that explain the observed 
increased ADHD risk among boys who were circumcised after the neonatal period. 
 
 
 
References 
1. Ing C, Sun M, Olfson M, et al. Age at exposure to surgery and anesthesia in children and association with 
mental disorder diagnosis. Anesth Analg 2017;125:1988-98. 
2. Ing C, Ma X, Sun M, et al. Exposure to surgery and anesthesia in early childhood and subsequent use of 
attention deficit hyperactivity disorder medications. Anesth Analg 2020;131:723-33. 
3. Owings M, Uddin S, Williams S. Trends in circumcision for male newborns in U.S. hospitals: 1979-2010. 
National Center for Health Statistics website. 
https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/circumcision_2013/circumcision_2013.pdf. Accessed October 14, 
2020. 
4. Wiswell TE, Tencer HL, Welch CA, Chamberlain JL. Circumcision in children beyond the neonatal period. 
Pediatrics 1993;92:791-3. 
 
 
