INSERT OR REPLACE INTO archive_documents (id, title, source_collection, url, type, status, content, metadata_json, created_at) VALUES ('van-howe-response-to-vogelstein-2012aap-bioethics-2018', 'Van Howe Response to Vogelstein 2012AAP  Bioethics 2018', 'Published Canon: Pediatric Complications & Epidemiology', '/documents/van-howe-response-to-vogelstein-2012aap-bioethics-2018.pdf', 'medical_journal', 'ingested', 'R E S P O N S E
Response to Vogelstein: How the 2012 AAP Task Force on
circumcision went wrong
Robert S. Van Howe
Correspondence
Robert S. Van Howe, Central Michigan
University College of Medicine 1000
Houghton Avenue, Saginaw, Michigan
48602, USA
E-mail: rsvanhowe@att.net,
Vanho1rs@cmich.edu
Abstract
Vogelstein cautions medical organizations against jumping into the fray of controversial issues, yet
proffers the 2012 American Academy of Pediatrics’ Task Force policy position on infant male circum-
cision as ‘an appropriate use of position-statements.’ Only a scratch below the surface of this policy
statement uncovers the Task Force’s failure to consider Vogelstein’s many caveats. The Task Force
supported the cultural practice by putting undeserved emphasis on questionable scientific data, while
ignoring or underplaying the importance of valid contrary scientific data. Without any effort to quanti-
tatively assess the risk/benefit balance, the Task Force concluded the benefits of circumcision
outweighed the risks, while acknowledging that the incidence of risks was unknown. This Task Force
differed from other Academy policy-forming panels by ignoring the Academy’s standard quality meas-
ures and by not appointing members with extensive research experience, extensive publications, or
recognized expertise directly related to this topic. Despite nearly 100 publications available at the
time addressing the substantial ethical issues associated with infant male circumcision, the Task Force
chose to ignore the ethical controversy. They merely stated, with minimal justification, the opinion of
one of the Task Force members that the practice of infant male circumcision is morally permissible.
The release of the report has fostered an explosion of academic discussion on the ethics of infant
male circumcision with a number of national medical organizations now decrying the practice as a
human rights violation.
K E Y W O R D S
circumcision, cultural bias, policy statement
I read with interest the recent article by Vogelstein cautioning medical
organizations against jumping into the fray of controversial issues.1 I
was struck that the author would hold out the American Academy of
Pediatrics (AAP) 2012 position on male circumcision2 as ‘an appropriate
use of position-statements,’ because when one looks carefully at the
report, a very different conclusion should be reached.
The author is correct in stating ‘This position is based largely on sci-
entific data,’ but the author may not be aware of the now well-
documented selective gathering and selective use of scientific data
employed by the Task Force. To reach their conclusion that ‘the benefits
of newborn male circumcision outweigh the risks,’ the Task Force gave
undeserved emphasis to scientific data that supported this conclusion
while ignoring a large swath of scientific data that do not support this
conclusion and ignored actual, substantial ethical controversies. For
example, the Task Force refused to consider any case reports or case-
series in their analysis. Since the most serious complications associated
with circumcision (including deaths) are primarily reported as case
reports or in case-series, these risks would be underestimated both sub-
jectively and objectively. Next, studies with results that presented cir-
cumcision in a less than favorable light were either excluded or, in many
cases, erroneously judged to be of lower quality. For example, among
studies of similar designs the Task Force systematically assigned higher
quality ratings to studies that were favorable to circumcision than to
those that were not favorable.3 In fact, the Task Force chose not to use
1Vogelstein, E. (2016). Professional hubris and its consequences: Why
organizations of health-care professions should not adopt ethically contro-
versial positions. Bioethics, 30(4), 234–243.
2Task Force on Circumcision. (2012). Male circumcision. Pediatrics, 130(3),
e756–e785.
3One example, out of many, is the Task Force’s assessment of the quality of
studies assessing the frequency of adverse outcomes. Based on study design,
studies that prospectively collect data are considered to be more reliable
than retrospective chart reviews, which in turn are considered to be more
reliable than studies based on information extracted from databases. The
Bioethics. 2018;32:77–80.
wileyonlinelibrary.com/journal/bioe
V
