|
Accepted: 28 February 2017
DOI: 10.1111/bioe.12363
bs_bs_banner


the standard quality assessment method that the AAP had adopted in
2004,4 but instead used a less standard quality assessment procedure.
In this manner the Task Force was able to formulate a conclusion that
was consistent with the prevailing cultural norm in the United States,
but which is inconsistent with that reached by all other peer medical
associations in other countries (see below). Unfortunately, such bias and
lack of scientific rigor may have put the AAP at risk for a lawsuit.5
While the AAP ‘plausibly has the scientific and clinical bona fides to
pronounce upon the issue’ and has a strong track record in making pro-
nouncements on other issues, the work of the 2012 Task Force falls
well below the AAP’s typical high standard.
When the AAP released policy statements on sinus infections, ear
infections, and urinary tract infections, the membership of the commit-
tees included experts with vast research experience, extensive publica-
tions, and expertise directly related to these topics.6 The publication
history on the topic of circumcision for the eight members of 2012 Cir-
cumcision Task Force included one original research study published in
1987,7 and three opinion pieces,8 one of which may have been solicited
because of the author’s membership on the Task Force.9 One of the
Task Force members, Dr. Andrew Freedman, stated in an interview that
he had circumcised his own son on his parents’ kitchen table, ‘not for
medical reasons . .. but because I had 3,000 years of ancestors looking
over my shoulder.’10 Another of the Task Force members was the Chair
of the Committee on Bioethics that concluded that some forms of rit-
ualized female genital cutting were ethically permissible without any
discussion of a child’s right to bodily integrity, even though all forms of
nontherapeutic female genital cutting are banned by federal law.11 In
the firestorm that ensued, the AAP ‘retired’ this position a month
later.12 With a steadfast proponent of parental authority, rather than
children’s interests, as the Committee on Bioethics’s representative to
the Task Force,13 it was unlikely that any serious, let alone nuanced, dis-
cussion of the child’s right to bodily integrity would take place.
Instead of acknowledging, confronting, and discussing the ongoing
controversy surrounding the moral permissibility of infant male circumci-
sion,14 the Task Force blithely acted as though there was no controversy
by simply failing to mention opposing views. This approach back-fired,
however, as the discussion about whether or not infant male circumci-
sion is morally permissible or even a human rights violation has exploded
since the AAP reports with several national medical organizations
characterising it as an impermissible violation of children’s rights.15
Such one-sidedness does not promote the constructive debate Vogel-
stein believes should be aimed for. As the author notes ‘greater and more
rigorous reflection and dialogue is more likely to result in the truth.’ The
evidence suggests, however, that the Task Force was not interested in the
‘truth,’ but rather in appealing to their own authority to use this policy-
statement as, what the author calls, an ‘argumentative bludgeon’ to lever-
age financial reimbursement from Medicaid and other third-party payers
for performing the procedure. Indeed, the repeated solicitation for funding
for circumcision throughout the report comes across as a non-sequitur.
prospective study cited by the Task Force documented a relatively high inci-
dence of adhesions, balanitis, and meatal stenosis following circumcision and
was assessed to be of ‘poor’ quality by the Task Force. The cited chart
review view study, undertaken by the Centers for Disease Control and Pre-
vention, reported a 3.2% rate of adverse outcomes and was assigned a ‘fair’
rating. The cited study based on database information reported a 0.2% rate
of adverse outcomes and was awarded a ‘good’ rating. These relative ratings
are the exact opposite of what would be expected based on study design
but exactly what would be expected if one had a bias toward demonstrating
that infant male circumcision is a low risk procedure. Christakis, D. A., et al.
(2000). A trade-off analysis of routine newborn circumcision. Pediatrics, 105
(1), 246–249; O’Brien, T. R., Calle, E. E., & Poole, W. K. (1995). Incidence of
neonatal circumcision in Atlanta, 1985–1986. Southern Medical Journal. 88(4),
411–415; Van Howe RS. (1997). Variability in penile appearance and penile
findings: A prospective study. British Journal of Urology, 80(5), 776–782.
