VAN HOWE


If the members of the Task Force were ‘experts in ways directly rel-
evant to determining the risks and benefits of procedures like circumci-
sion, in particular through clinical experience and by evaluating and
putting into context relevant scientific evidence,’ then why does the
AAP stand alone in its conclusion? National medical and pediatric organi-
zations in Canada, Australasia, the Netherlands, Germany, Slovenia,
Sweden, Norway, Denmark, Finland, and South Africa, along with the
Nordic Ombudsmen, and the Council of Europe all had access to the
same body of knowledge as the members of the AAP Task Force, yet
did not reach the same conclusion.16
It is striking that Vogelstein would mention only in passing that the
AAP policy statement ‘has its detractors,’ without citing any of the rele-
vant criticisms or contrary policies.17 With the AAP standing so far
afield from the rest of the world,18 one wonders whether the members
of the 2012 Task Force were privy to information or insights that the
majority of their peers and counterparts in other nations are missing?
Most unsettling is that their conclusion, ‘that the health benefits of
newborn male circumcision outweigh the risks’ does not stand on its
own logical feet. If the benefits outweigh the risks, then the Task Force
should have recommended the procedure, but the Task Force does not
recommend the procedure. Not surprisingly, circumcision proponents
have used the Task Force’s pronouncement that the benefits outweigh
the risks as a tool to promote circumcision, often failing to mention, as
the author does, that the Task Force failed to recommend the
procedure.
Furthermore, the report does not directly compare the benefits
and risks because it was impossible for them to do so. Several times in
the report the Task Force acknowledges that ‘[t]he true incidence of
complications after newborn circumcision is unknown.’ One can take
this a step further, as Darby has, by observing that the Task Force only
tallied the ‘complications’ following the procedure without considering
the intrinsic harms/losses associated with losing a heathy and func-
tional body part.19 This lack of knowledge could result in a failure to
recognize the procedure’s intrinsic harms/losses. If the risks and harms
of the procedure have not been quantified, how did the Task Force
determine that they were outweighed by the benefits? Moreover, how
and on what basis did the Task Force assign values or weights to indi-
vidual risks and benefits, so that they could be meaningfully balanced
against one another? Frisch and Earp note that this cannot be done by
the fiat of a committee:
consider the example of a modest reduction in the absolute
risk of contracting certain sexually transmitted infections, a
