they will depend upon such factors as how much value one
places on having intact versus modified genitalia, how willing
one is to engage in safe sex practices (even if one is circum-
cised), and how much risk one feels comfortable taking on
when it comes to a surgery performed on a physically and sym-
bolically sensitive part of one’s body.20
Indeed, in a later editorial, Task Force member Andrew Freedman
conceded the point: in addition to having ‘insufficient information
about the actual incidence and burden of nonacute complications,’ he
wrote, the AAP’s 2012 assessment of benefits versus risks was also
hampered by the ‘lack of a universally accepted metric to accurately
measure or balance the risks and benefits.’21
Even the metrics that have been devised were not used. The Task
Force showed no evidence of performing a cost analysis, a cost-benefit
analysis, a cost-effectiveness analysis, or a cost-utility analysis. Instead,
they reached their conclusion without providing any direct comparison
of the benefits and risks whatsoever. When confronted with this short-
coming,22 the AAP backpedaled by stating, ‘These benefits were felt to
outweigh the risks of the procedure.’23
Is it ‘an appropriate use of position-statements’ when a policy is
based on the subjective feelings of an 8-member committee rather
than scientific evidence in the apparent service of cultural or other
non-scientific objectives? As Freedman stated in his editorial:
Most circumcisions are done due to religious and cultural tradi-
tion. In the West, although parents may use the conflicting
medical literature to buttress their own beliefs and desires, for
the most part parents choose what they want for a wide vari-
ety of nonmedical reasons. There can be no doubt that religion,
culture, aesthetic preference, familial identity, and personal
experience all factor into their decision [. . .] Given the role of
the phallus in our culture, it is not illegitimate to consider these
realms of a person’s life in making this nontherapeutic, only
partially medical decision. . . . The ethical standard [we] used
was ‘the best interest of the child,’ and in this setting the well-
informed parent was felt to be the best proxy to pass this judg-
ment. Protecting this option was not an idle concern at a time
when there are serious efforts in both the United States and
Europe to ban the procedure outright.24
Thus, Freedman makes clear that the AAP Task Force was not
engaged in a dispassionate exercise in assessing and balancing medical
benefits and risks, but was set on reaching a conclusion that was aimed
at blatantly non-scientific ends ... precisely the sort of thing that Vogel-
stein is objecting to in his article. In conclusion, the stance taken by the
AAP on male circumcision was not a success but a failure. It did not
learn its lesson from the failed female genital cutting policy of 2010. It
did not give guidance to North American physicians who, like the major-
ity of their European colleagues, believe infant male circumcision is a
human rights violation. The AAP would have been much better off issu-
ing a policy on the care of intact genitals in males and to begin develop-
ing a strategy for how distance itself from a cultural practice that is
widely regarded as harmful outside of the communities that are invested
in it, and is rejected by majority of the world. Instead, they took a reac-
tionary approach of cultural relativism and circled their wagons.
ORCID
Robert S. Van Howe
http://orcid.org/0000-0003-4493-3585
AUTHOR BIOGRAPHY
ROBERT S. VAN HOWE received his M.D. from Loyola University Stritch
School of Medicine and his M.S. in Clinical Research Design and Statis-
tical Analysis from the University of Michigan School of Public Health.
He is currently Professor and Chief of Pediatrics at Central Michigan
University College of Medicine.
