NOTE: This is the draft version of an article that was accepted for publication into the (forthcoming) Spring 2016 issue of 
The International Journal of Children''s Rights.
This article asks the manifestly important question of whether the draft CDC circumcision 
recommendations are medically,6 ethically,7 legally,8 and procedurally justified.9  If not, what are 
the implications; can the draft recommendations be challenged; and what recommendations 
should the CDC be making about circumcision to improve the safety and health of boys and 
men?10
THE DRAFT CDC RECOMMENDATIONS.  According to the draft CDC recommendations, 
physicians should inform the parents of every newborn boy in the United States and all 
heterosexually active uncircumcised men as follows:  (1) Circumcision has many medical 
benefits11—including a reduced risk of urinary tract infections (UTIs), penile cancer, and 
sexually transmitted infections (STIs), especially a "dramatic" and "significant" 50% to 60% 
reduction in the risk of female-to-male transmission of HIV12—and these benefits outweigh the 
risks.13  (2) Parents have the right to make the "circumcision decision" for their sons, and in doing 
so should take into consideration, in addition to the health benefits and risks, religion, societal 
norms and customs, aesthetic preference, and hygiene.14  (3) The benefits of circumcision justify 
insurance paying for it, including the joint federal and state Medicaid insurance program.15  
According to the draft CDC recommendations, neonatal circumcision has few disadvantages.  
Analgesia can substantially control pain.16  The complication rate is approximately 0.2%.17  The 
most common surgical complications, bleeding and infection, are usually minor and easily 
managed.18  Post-surgical complications are rare.19  Circumcision also leaves sexual function or 
satisfaction unchanged or improves it.20  
