Letter to the Editor
Letter to the Editor on “The impact of elective adult 
circumcision for religious reasons on sexual function and
satisfaction: a pilot study”
Tim Hammond* 
Genital Autonomy Legal Defense and Education Fund, Palm Springs, CA 92262, United States
*Corresponding author: Genital Autonomy Legal Defense and Education Fund, Palm Springs, CA 92262, United States.
Email: circharmsurvey@gmail.com 
Fihman et al. (2026) examined sexual function and satis-
faction following elective adult circumcision for religious 
reasons, contributing data where empirical research remains
limited.1 The observations noted below may help contextual-
ize those findings.
One factor that may have influenced responses is the inter-
action between religious commitment and postoperative self-
evaluation. In general, some individuals may experience social 
or internal pressures that shape how they report satisfaction 
or regret following elective medical procedures. This phe-
nomenon has been described i n the broader literature on deci-
sion regret across various elective surgeries. Contextualizing
how religious or cultural expectations can influence post-
circumcision self-report can help readers better understand the
study’s outcomes.
While no major postoperative complications were reported 
among the adult participants, the authors later reference 
neonatal circumcision. Adult and neonatal procedures differ 
significantly in terms of anatomical development and con-
sent. Small surgical errors during infant circumcision can
become magnified in adolescence and adulthood, resulting in
unpredictable adverse physical and/or sexual consequences.2 
The involuntary nature of newborn circumcision has been 
identified by numerous researchers to be a contributor to later
emotional or psychological distress.3 
The cited 2012 American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) 
Circumcision Task Force statement concluded that benefits 
slightly outweighed risks, but the policy curiously expired in 
2017 without being renewed or reaffirmed. In a November 
2025 interview, two distinguished members of that AAP Cir-
cumcision Task Force essentially disavowed the validity of its
findings.4 
Andrew Freedman, MD described neonatal circumcision as 
a non-therapeutic procedure, remarking “If it can be called a 
preventative medicine, it is at the very weakest level.”Douglas 
Diekema, MD declared “When you look at all the data, I
don’t think you can honestly say in a recommendation that
the benefits outweigh the risks.”
These comments are intended to inspire constructive dia-
logue and to encourage more robust medical, ethical, and legal 
research toward safeguarding healthy unconsenting minors 
from the increasingly documented harms of nontherapeutic
circumcision, while protecting their fundamental human right
to bodily integrity, including genital autonomy.
Funding 
None declared. 
Disclosure 
The Genital Autonomy Legal Defense and Education Fund (GALDEF) 
is a nonprofit civil society organization advocating for improved pedi-
atric practice and medical e thics policies that recognize every child’s
basic human right to bodily integrity, including genital autonomy.
