INSERT OR REPLACE INTO archive_documents (id, title, source_collection, url, type, status, content, metadata_json, created_at) VALUES ('sneppen-foreskin-morbidity-peds-2016', 'Sneppen Foreskin morbidity Peds 2016', 'Published Canon: Medical Policy & Bioethics', '/documents/sneppen-foreskin-morbidity-peds-2016.pdf', 'medical_journal', 'ingested', 'ARTICLE
PEDIATRICS Volume  137 , number  5 ,  May 2016 :e 20154340 
Foreskin Morbidity in 
Uncircumcised Males
Ida Sneppen, MD, a Jørgen Thorup, MD, PhDa, b
abstract
OBJECTIVE: As a consequence of the discussion on whether the health benefits of newborn 
male circumcision outweigh the risks and the discrepancies in reported figures of 
complications, we evaluated the incidence and morbidity of foreskin surgery due to medical 
indications in boys from the Capital Region of Denmark in 2014.
METHODS: Medical records from all boys operated on the foreskin due to medical reasons in 
the Capital Region in 2014 were reviewed. Patients with hypospadias, ritual circumcision, 
and redo-surgery because of complications to nontherapeutic circumcision were excluded.
RESULTS: A total of 181 patients were included. The cumulative risk of undergoing foreskin 
operation before 18 years of age was 1.7%. Forty patients had histologic verified balanitis 
xerotica obliterans (BXO) corresponding to a total risk of 0.37% of developing BXO. Mean 
age at surgery was 10.1 years (range 1–17). Phimosis was the most frequently reported 
indication (95.0%). The remaining 5.0% underwent surgery because of frenulum breve 
causing problems during erection. Before surgery, 27.1% had foreskin-related voiding 
problems and 17.1% had at least 1 episode of balanitis. Circumcision was initially 
performed in 44 cases. The remaining 137 patients had a foreskin-preserving operation 
performed. Nine boys had secondary circumcision after initially having foreskin-preserving 
operation. Fifty patients initially had preputial histology performed. BXO was verified in 
37 patients. Of the 9 patients with redo-surgery due to recurrent phimosis, a further 3 had 
histologically verified BXO.
CONCLUSIONS: Childhood foreskin-related problems in a region with no tradition of newborn 
male circumcision should not be neglected.
 aDepartment of Pediatric Surgery, Surgical Clinic C, Rigshospitalet, University Hospital of Copenhagen, 
Copenhagen, Denmark; and bFaculty of Health and Medical Sciences, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, 
Denmark
Dr Sneppen carried out the initial analyses and drafted the initial manuscript; Dr Thorup 
conceptualized and designed the study, and reviewed and revised the manuscript; and both 
authors approved the ﬁ nal manuscript as submitted and agree to be accountable for all aspects 
of the work.
DOI: 10.1542/peds.2015-4340
Accepted for publication Feb 10, 2016
 Address correspondence to Jørgen Thorup, MD, PhD, 4272 Department of Pediatric Surgery, 
Rigshospitalet, 9 Blegdamsvej, DK-2100 Copenhagen, Denmark. E-mail: j-thorup@rh.dk
